<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd"><key id="d13" for="edge" attr.name="target_phenomenon" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d12" for="edge" attr.name="source_phenomenon" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="relation_type" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="semantic_rationale" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="scale_direction" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="edge_id" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="units" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="mathematical_definition" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="description" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d4" for="node" attr.name="phenomenon_id" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d3" for="node" attr.name="scale" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d2" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="schema_version" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="evidence_status" attr.type="string"/>
<graph edgedefault="directed"><data key="d0">unvalidated_semantic_candidates</data>
<data key="d1">2.0</data>
<node id="sic_micro_prior_setting_degree">
  <data key="d2">Newly infected agent prior same-setting contact degree</data>
  <data key="d3">micro</data>
  <data key="d4">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d5">Per infection event, number of distinct partners the infected agent had in the event's contact layer during a pre-infection observation window.</data>
  <data key="d6">For each event row e in events with run_id r, step t, agent_id a, layer L, classified as an infection transition by a fixed observed event_type/state label set, define D(e)=# { p : p is non-null/non-sentinel, p!=a, and exists c in contacts with c.run_id=r, c.layer=L, c.step in [t-W,t-1], and ((c.agent_id=a and c.partner_id=p) or (c.partner_id=a and c.agent_id=p)) }. The indicator value for e is D(e). W is a pre-specified nonnegative integer window length.</data>
  <data key="d7">contacts per infection event</data>
</node>
<node id="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d2">Layer-specific infection events per contact edge</data>
  <data key="d3">meso</data>
  <data key="d4">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d5">For each run, contact layer, and time window, ratio of infection-transition events to observed contact rows in that layer.</data>
  <data key="d6">For run r, layer L, window [s,s+W-1], let I(r,L,s)=# { e in events : e.run_id=r, e.layer=L, e.step in [s,s+W-1], e classified as an infection transition }, and C(r,L,s)=# { c in contacts : c.run_id=r, c.layer=L, c.step in [s,s+W-1] }. Indicator = I(r,L,s)/C(r,L,s) when C&gt;0; undefined when C=0 or L is null.</data>
  <data key="d7">infection events per contact row</data>
</node>
<node id="sic_macro_layer_entropy">
  <data key="d2">City infection-event layer entropy</data>
  <data key="d3">macro</data>
  <data key="d4">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d5">Normalized entropy of infection-transition events across observed contact layers for each run and time window.</data>
  <data key="d6">For run r and window [s,s+W-1], let n_L = # infection-transition events with layer L for each non-null layer label L observed in r; N=sum_L n_L; p_L=n_L/N; K=# non-null layer labels observed in r in contacts or events; H = -(sum_L p_L log p_L)/log K if N&gt;0 and K&gt;1; undefined otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d7">dimensionless normalized entropy</data>
</node>
<node id="icts_micro_quarantine_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d2">Agent contact-degree ratio around quarantine onset</data>
  <data key="d3">micro</data>
  <data key="d4">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d5">Per quarantine-onset event, ratio of distinct contacts after onset to distinct contacts before onset for the same agent.</data>
  <data key="d6">For each event q in events with run_id r, step s, agent_id a, state matching the observed quarantined label, before=false, after=true, let P_pre={p: exists c in contacts, c.run_id=r, c.step in [s-W,s-1], ((c.agent_id=a and c.partner_id=p) or (c.partner_id=a and c.agent_id=p)), p non-null/non-sentinel, p!=a} and P_post similarly for c.step in [s+1,s+W]. Indicator = |P_post|/|P_pre| if |P_pre|&gt;0; undefined if |P_pre|=0.</data>
  <data key="d7">dimensionless ratio</data>
</node>
<node id="icts_meso_layer_contact_change">
  <data key="d2">Layer contact-volume change around quarantine-count increase</data>
  <data key="d3">meso</data>
  <data key="d4">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d5">Median within-layer change in mean per-step contact rows around steps where the model quarantine count increases.</data>
  <data key="d6">For run r and layer L, let Q_t=model_states.quarantined at step t. Let S={t: Q_t&gt;Q_prev(t) with previous sampled step observed}. For each t in S, define C_pre(t)=mean over u in [t-W,t-1] intersect observed steps of # contacts rows with run_id=r, layer=L, step=u; C_post(t)=mean over u in [t+1,t+W] intersect observed steps of # contacts rows with run_id=r, layer=L, step=u. Indicator = median over t in S of (C_post(t)-C_pre(t)) when S and required pre/post steps exist; undefined if S is empty or no pre/post observed steps exist.</data>
  <data key="d7">contact rows per step</data>
</node>
<node id="icts_macro_city_contact_change_on_quarantine_increase">
  <data key="d2">City contact-volume change on quarantine-increase steps</data>
  <data key="d3">macro</data>
  <data key="d4">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d5">Difference in mean step-to-step change of total city contact rows between steps with positive quarantine-count change and steps with zero quarantine-count change.</data>
  <data key="d6">For run r, let C_t=# contacts rows at step t; DeltaC_t=C_t-C_{t-1}; DeltaQ_t=Q_t-Q_{t-1} from model_states.quarantined. Let A=mean{DeltaC_t: DeltaQ_t&gt;0 and both t and t-1 are observed}; B=mean{DeltaC_t: DeltaQ_t=0 and both t and t-1 are observed}. Indicator=A-B. Undefined if A or B has no members.</data>
  <data key="d7">contact rows per step</data>
</node>
<edge source="sic_micro_prior_setting_degree" target="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d8">upward__edge_sic_micro_degree_to_meso_layer_ratio</data>
  <data key="d9">upward</data>
  <data key="d10">Infected agents with higher prior same-setting contact degree are more embedded in dense layer-specific contact structures; their subsequent interactions can generate more same-layer infection events per observed contact row, shifting the meso layer infection-to-contact ratio beyond simple event aggregation.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sic_micro_prior_setting_degree" target="sic_macro_layer_entropy">
  <data key="d8">upward__edge_sic_micro_degree_to_macro_layer_entropy</data>
  <data key="d9">upward</data>
  <data key="d10">Heterogeneous prior contact embeddedness of infected agents across settings can create layer-specific infection clustering; when high-degree infections concentrate in particular layers, the city-wide infection-event distribution across layers can become more skewed, altering normalized layer entropy.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sic_micro_prior_setting_degree" target="icts_micro_quarantine_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d8">intra_scale__micro_prior_degree_to_quarantine_ratio</data>
  <data key="d9">intra_scale</data>
  <data key="d10">A newly infected agent's prior same-setting contact degree can shape how many contacts are available for notification and how the agent's behavior changes when later quarantined; greater prior connectedness may imply a larger pre-onset contact set and a different potential post-onset contact reduction, thereby affecting the observed post/pre contact ratio.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d13">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio" target="sic_macro_layer_entropy">
  <data key="d8">upward__edge_sic_meso_layer_ratio_to_macro_layer_entropy</data>
  <data key="d9">upward</data>
  <data key="d10">Differential infection-to-contact intensity across layers changes the relative share of infection events attributed to each layer; an elevated ratio in one layer can make city infection events more concentrated, while more balanced ratios across layers can diversify the city-level layer distribution.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio" target="sic_micro_prior_setting_degree">
  <data key="d8">downward__layer_infection_ratio_to_micro_prior_degree</data>
  <data key="d9">downward</data>
  <data key="d10">A layer with a higher infection-event-per-contact ratio presents a more intense exposure environment; agents infected in that layer plausibly had more prior distinct partners because repeated exposure opportunities increase infection likelihood.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio" target="icts_meso_layer_contact_change">
  <data key="d8">intra_scale__meso_infection_ratio_to_layer_contact_change</data>
  <data key="d9">intra_scale</data>
  <data key="d10">A layer with more infection events per observed contact row can trigger more detection, tracing, and quarantine activity associated with that setting; when city quarantine counts rise, this can produce larger reductions in contact volume within that layer because more agents linked to the layer are removed or reduce activity.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d13">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sic_macro_layer_entropy" target="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d8">downward__macro_entropy_to_layer_infection_ratio</data>
  <data key="d9">downward</data>
  <data key="d10">Higher citywide layer entropy indicates infection events are spread across many activity layers; this changes which settings carry infectious persons, plausibly diluting or redistributing per-layer infection pressure and thereby affecting layer-specific infection events per contact edge.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sic_macro_layer_entropy" target="icts_macro_city_contact_change_on_quarantine_increase">
  <data key="d8">intra_scale__macro_layer_entropy_to_city_contact_change</data>
  <data key="d9">intra_scale</data>
  <data key="d10">A more dispersed or concentrated distribution of infection events across layers indicates which settings are driving transmission; this changes which settings generate quarantine increases and how city-level contact volume changes when quarantine counts rise.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">setting_infection_clustering</data>
  <data key="d13">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_micro_quarantine_contact_ratio" target="icts_meso_layer_contact_change">
  <data key="d8">upward__edge_icts_micro_quarantine_ratio_to_meso_layer_contact_change</data>
  <data key="d9">upward</data>
  <data key="d10">When quarantined agents retain a larger share of their pre-onset contacts, layer-level contact volume around quarantine-count increases declines less; widespread low post/pre contact ratios instead amplify layer contact-volume contraction.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_micro_quarantine_contact_ratio" target="icts_macro_city_contact_change_on_quarantine_increase">
  <data key="d8">upward__edge_icts_micro_quarantine_ratio_to_macro_city_contact_change</data>
  <data key="d9">upward</data>
  <data key="d10">Individual contact retention after quarantine onset attenuates the aggregate city contact-volume decline on quarantine-increase steps; if many quarantined agents maintain contacts, the city-level differential contact change is less negative than when post-onset contacts drop sharply.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_micro_quarantine_contact_ratio" target="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d8">upward__edge_icts_micro_quarantine_ratio_to_meso_layer_infection_ratio</data>
  <data key="d9">upward</data>
  <data key="d10">Quarantined agents who retain more contacts can continue to create infectious contact opportunities within their layer, potentially raising layer infection events per contact row; strong post-onset contact reduction instead lowers that layer-specific infection-to-contact ratio.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_micro_quarantine_contact_ratio" target="sic_micro_prior_setting_degree">
  <data key="d8">intra_scale__micro_quarantine_to_prior_degree</data>
  <data key="d9">intra_scale</data>
  <data key="d10">Quarantine onset and the associated reduction in an agent's contacts remove that agent from ongoing setting-level mixing; this changes the pool and connectivity of remaining contacts available to susceptible agents, altering the prior same-setting contact degree observed for later infection events in those settings.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_meso_layer_contact_change" target="sic_macro_layer_entropy">
  <data key="d8">upward__edge_icts_meso_layer_contact_change_to_macro_layer_entropy</data>
  <data key="d9">upward</data>
  <data key="d10">Quarantine-associated contact-volume contractions that differ by layer redistribute mixing opportunities among settings, which can shift transmission toward less-contracted layers and thereby reshape the city-level distribution of infection events across layers.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_meso_layer_contact_change" target="icts_micro_quarantine_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d8">downward__layer_contact_change_to_micro_quarantine_ratio</data>
  <data key="d9">downward</data>
  <data key="d10">Contraction of contact volume in a layer after quarantine-count increases reduces the partners available there; quarantined agents whose activity is concentrated in that layer may show a lower post/pre contact-degree ratio.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_meso_layer_contact_change" target="sic_micro_prior_setting_degree">
  <data key="d8">downward__layer_contact_change_to_micro_prior_degree</data>
  <data key="d9">downward</data>
  <data key="d10">Layer-specific contact contraction around quarantine increases lowers the opportunity to meet distinct partners in that setting; newly infected agents in that layer plausibly have fewer prior distinct contacts before infection.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_meso_layer_contact_change" target="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d8">intra_scale__meso_layer_contact_change_to_infection_ratio</data>
  <data key="d9">intra_scale</data>
  <data key="d10">Quarantine-associated reductions in a layer's contact volume change the density and mixing pattern of remaining contacts; this alters both the contact-row denominator and the likelihood that infection events occur per remaining contact row within that layer.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_macro_city_contact_change_on_quarantine_increase" target="icts_micro_quarantine_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d8">downward__macro_city_contact_to_micro_quarantine_ratio</data>
  <data key="d9">downward</data>
  <data key="d10">A citywide fall in contact rows on quarantine-increase steps creates a lower-opportunity contact environment; quarantined agents' post-onset contacts may fall relative to their pre-onset contacts, lowering the individual post/pre contact-degree ratio.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_macro_city_contact_change_on_quarantine_increase" target="sic_micro_prior_setting_degree">
  <data key="d8">downward__macro_city_contact_to_micro_prior_degree</data>
  <data key="d9">downward</data>
  <data key="d10">Citywide contact contraction coincident with quarantine increases reduces the pool of available partners, so newly infected agents are plausibly less able to accumulate many distinct prior partners in their infection setting.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_macro_city_contact_change_on_quarantine_increase" target="sic_meso_layer_infection_contact_ratio">
  <data key="d8">downward__macro_city_contact_to_layer_infection_ratio</data>
  <data key="d9">downward</data>
  <data key="d10">When citywide contacts contract on quarantine-increase steps, remaining contacts may concentrate in essential or household layers; this selective contact regime can raise or alter infection events per contact in those layers.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="icts_macro_city_contact_change_on_quarantine_increase" target="sic_macro_layer_entropy">
  <data key="d8">intra_scale__macro_city_contact_change_to_layer_entropy</data>
  <data key="d9">intra_scale</data>
  <data key="d10">City contact-volume changes on quarantine-increase steps can reduce contacts unevenly across layers; by suppressing mixing in some settings more than others, these changes reshape the relative occurrence of infection events across settings and therefore the layer entropy of infection events.</data>
  <data key="d11">semantic_candidate</data>
  <data key="d12">intervention_contact_trajectory_shift</data>
  <data key="d13">setting_infection_clustering</data>
</edge>
</graph></graphml>